<title>UK: UK's Clarke says no change in position on EMU.</title>
<headline>UK's Clarke says no change in position on EMU.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-02-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Chancellor of the Exchequer Kenneth Clarke said comments on economic and monetary union (EMU) by Foreign Malcolm Rifkind did not represent a change in the British government's policy.</p>
<p>&quot;The foreign secretary wasn't intending to change the position of the government,&quot; Clarke told reporters.</p>
<p>Rifkind, speaking earlier on BBC radio, said the government was not neutral on EMU. On balance, he said, it was hostile to the single currency planned for launch in 1999.</p>
<p>Clarke said Rifkind's comments appeared to be a slip of the tongue under pressure from a skilful interviewer.</p>
<p>-- London newsroom 44-171-542 7767</p>
</text>
